//! A module of **parameterised types and procedures** — named `Generic_Types` rather than `Generic`, because
//! `tests/corpus/modules/Generic.jr` already exists (it declares the *templates* ADR-0104 refuses to instantiate
//! across a file) and a stdlib module shadowing a fixture module made a corpus file resolve the wrong one. A
//! module name is a global identifier in the search path, so a new one must be checked against both trees.
//!
//! Its structs were exported for `valid/097` — the first library module whose types are generic across a
//! module boundary (ADR-0117). ADR-0230 adds the corresponding pure `$T` procedure surface.
//!
//! Until that ADR a `struct($T)` here was unusable by every importer, which is why `Array`, `List` and `Map` are
//! concrete `Int_*` types. This module exists to *exercise* the capability, deliberately separate from converting
//! those three: the language change and a library rewrite are different work, and keeping them apart is what
//! makes a regression in either attributable.

/// Returns `value` unchanged.
///
/// This is the smallest pure `$T` procedure an importer can specialize, and keeps the cross-file procedure
/// fixture beside the parameterised types it is intended to compose with.
identity :: (value: $T) -> T {
  return value;
}

/// A single value of any type — the smallest parameterised struct, and the shape ADR-0085 used to introduce them.
Box :: struct($T) {
  /// The value.
  value: T;
}

/// Two values of independent types.
///
/// Two parameters rather than one, because a list of arguments has to be genuinely a list: an implementation that
/// bound only the first would pass every one-parameter test.
Pair :: struct($A, $B) {
  /// The first.
  first: A;
  /// The second.
  second: B;
}
